+<div style='mso-element:endnote' id=edn98>
+
+<p class=MsoNormal style='mso-pagination:none;mso-layout-grid-align:none;
+text-autospace:none'><a style='mso-endnote-id:edn98' href="#_ednref98"
+name="_edn98" title=""><span class=MsoEndnoteReference>9</span></a><sup>8 </sup><span
+lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>thn uper autou
+dunamin</span>. The Ben. version simplifies this difficult expression by
+ignoring the <span lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>uper</span>.
+Mr. E. N. Bennett has suggested to me that the true reading may be <span
+lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>uperaulon</span> for <span
+lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>iper autou</span> (<span
+lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>aulo&quot;</span>
+supra 8. 1, <span lang=EL style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>uperaulw&quot;</span>
+in Philo). I would add the suggestion that <span lang=EL style='font-family:
+GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>autou</span> stood after <span lang=EL
+style='font-family:GraecaII;mso-ansi-language:EL'>uperaulon</span>, and that
+the similarity of the five letters in <span style='font-variant:small-caps'>ms.</span>
+caused the second word to be dropped out. <i>&#8216;His&#8217; exceeding immaterial power</i>
+would be the resulting sense. (See Class. Review, 1890, No. iv. p. 182.) </p>
+
+</div>
