+</span><br/><span id="fn243"><a href="#r243">243</a>&nbsp;ouk endiafhkouz men, alla kai antilegomenaj . Eusebius, in this clause, refers to the noqoi noqoi and antilegomena interchangeably (as e.g. in chap. 31, §6). In the present passage the noqoi , as both uncanonical and disputed, are distinguished from the canonical writings,-including both the universally accepted and the disputed, - which are here thrown together without distinction. The point to be emphasized is that he is separating here the uncanonical from the canonical, without regard to the character of the individual writings within the latter class.
